  <title>7.8.4: Writing Equivalent Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01863</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.8.4: Writing Equivalent Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>093fafcc-c05e-4703-b393-4cbcbb97b0a6</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Draw a diagram to show that \((3x+1)(x+2)\) is equivalent to \(3x^2+7x+2\).</item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Diagram should show partial products of \(3x(x)\), \(6x\), \(x\), and 2, which add up to \(3x^2+7x+2\).</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7cd83e7946410aa753c891045d8c29dbbf01b4cd"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Is \((x+4)^2\) equivalent to \(2x^2+8x+8\)? Explain or show your reasoning.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>No. \((x+4)(x+4)=x^2+4x+4x+16=x^2+8x+16\). </para></section>
</content>
